\begin{proof}
Let $h,g:\bar A\to Y$ be continuous extensions of $f$. Suppose, toward a contradiction, that $h(x)\ne g(x)$ for some $x\in\bar A$. Since $h=g=f$ on $A$, we have $x\notin A$. As $x\in\bar A$, every neighbourhood of $x$ meets $A$.

Because $Y$ is Hausdorff, there are disjoint neighbourhoods $U$ of $h(x)$ and $V$ of $g(x)$. By continuity, $W=h^{-1}(U)\cap g^{-1}(V)$ is a neighbourhood of $x$. Hence $W\cap A\ne\varnothing$; choose $y\in W\cap A$. Then $h(y)=g(y)$, while $h(y)\in U$ and $g(y)\in V$, so $h(y)\in U\cap V$, contradicting $U\cap V=\varnothing$. Therefore $h=g$ on $\bar A$, and the extension is unique.
\end{proof}
